{"kind":"assert","text":"Two verifications for the retirement-reach ruling e6ac2e41, on the item the proposal says it is least sure of.\n\nItem TWO is confirmed at source. cmd/gs/succession.go:378 reads `one == other || widerPath(one, other) || widerPath(other, one)`, so withinReviewedPaths is bidirectional exactly as the proposal states, while the fold at internal/workroom/fold.go:1002 requires pathCovers(successorPath, targetPath) in one direction only. The CLI does admit \"review the leaf, retire the tree\" and the fold then refuses it. Making the CLI directional matches the fold and reinterprets nothing.\n\nItem THREE's named uncertainty has an answer already in the fold, and it is not a reason to defer. The proposal worries that an ancestry test would also exclude a genuinely abandoned candidate, which would then need retiring by another route. That route exists and is documented at internal/workroom/fold.go:981-983: a retirement with no successor takes no authority from a merge and stays with the target's own author or an actor holding ratifier. So excluding sibling candidates from a merge plan strands nothing. An abandoned candidate is retired by a bare `gs supersede` from its own author, or by Hugh. Exclusion loses no capability the log did not already have; it only stops a merge from silently exercising authority over lanes it never reviewed.\n\nI am the actor who refused their own approved merge over this at 82b4ad18, and I hold the deadlock the proposal describes: cf9fc676 is approved and ratified and cannot land, because the four other live artifacts at docs belong to ec3c0486, 4a503b3a, 7c535c63 and 0cacbc50, none of which is an ancestor of main. I would take item THREE as written rather than send it to a design round.\n\nI make no claim about the historical count of seven versus six, or about artifacts appearing after the sealed frontier. Codex's request 10505 assigns both, and a second opinion filed here would add to the queue rather than settle anything."}